{
  "analysis": "The review describes a chance-based spin-wheel reward mechanic in the Genie game. The wheel can appear to stop on a valuable prize, but the game then moves the result to another spin and produces a substantially lower reward, reducing the player's apparent winnings. The spin wheel itself supports Reward Mania because rewards are delivered through an unpredictable repeated-attempt mechanism. The claimed movement of the wheel after it stops also describes alleged manipulation of outcome determination, supporting Illusion of Control. No real-money stake is explicitly described, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the genie game, in multiple times whe the wheel spin after stopping on a great price",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners; Variable Rewards",
      "rationale": "The game delivers prizes through a spin-wheel mechanic with repeated and variable reward outcomes."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price",
      "rule_applied": "Alleged rigging boundary: assign when the span asserts that the game manipulates the determination of outcomes.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game changes an apparent result after the wheel stops, implying that the outcome is manipulated rather than determined by the visible spin."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a 30-second matchmaking connection wait, but this is a service or matchmaking delay rather than an in-game timer that gates play, so it does not qualify as Playing by Appointment. It also describes weapon upgrades and perks as difficult to obtain, requiring the player to keep battling to win them. That identifies repeated gameplay activity being used to obtain progression-related upgrades, which supports Grinding. The statement that rewards are 'just for show' is a general complaint about reward value or presentation and does not identify a qualifying reward-delivery mechanic. No purchase, social, competition, or other dark-pattern mechanic is explicitly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Its difficult to get weapon upgrades or any percs others you stick battling to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Grinding definition and boundary rule vs Slow Progress: the span identifies battling as the repeated activity required to obtain progression-related upgrades.",
      "rationale": "The player must continue battling repeatedly to win weapon upgrades or perks, emphasizing repetitive effort for progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive matches against other players, including repeated use of specific cards and difficulty defending against them. It explicitly states that progression in trophies and winning are increasingly tied to spending money rather than skill, which supports Pay to Progress through the Pay to Win indicator. The claim that cards are buffed because players dislike them describes balance changes and frustration, but does not show that a purchased item lost value after acquisition, so Power Creep is not supported. The statement that players must be annoying to win does not establish an incentive for dishonest or harmful conduct beyond ordinary competitive play, and the complaint that skill does not determine advancement does not describe hidden outcome manipulation, so neither anti-social behavior nor Illusion of Control is assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes playing against other players in competitive matches."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The span states that gaining higher trophy progress requires spending money rather than relying only on skill."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a pass that the player is willing to purchase while advising against additional purchases, which supports the recurring-fee/battle-pass mechanic. It also explicitly describes trading lives with friends and a team, a resource exchange with existing players, supporting Reciprocity. The 9-diamond issue describes an in-game currency expenditure and an element failing to appear within a fixed number of moves, but it does not clearly establish real-money currency acquisition, gambling, waste of leftover currency, or a deliberate timer gate. The customer-service failure is not itself a game mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "I suggest after this cust. svc experience that you don't buy from this game beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes are an indicator of Recurring Fee. The search was used to resolve 'the pass' as Fishdom's Gold Pass/season-pass feature.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer identifies a pass as the one purchase they would retain while rejecting additional game purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives.",
      "rule_applied": "Reciprocity applies where players exchange resources, lives, or favors with people already playing.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes trading lives with friends and teammates."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Fishdom \"the pass\" diamonds 2023 game pass",
  "search_result": "The search established that Fishdom has a Gold Pass associated with seasonal rewards and that the pass expires at the end of a season. ([playrix.helpshift.com](https://playrix.helpshift.com/hc/en/26-fishdom-solitaire/faq/11213-season-adventures/?f=what-are-the-differences-between-cards&l=ru&p=android&s=zoo&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two current game mechanics despite presenting them as suggestions. First, lives are replenished on a real-time timer, so the player must wait for a new life before continuing; the requested 5–10 minute duration implies an existing wait-to-play schedule. Second, the game provides daily prizes and resets the accumulated daily reward progress when a day is missed. The increasing difficulty of levels and the reviewer’s positive evaluation do not independently support a label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers impose arbitrary wait times, including lives or energy refilling over time.",
      "rationale": "The suggestion implies that obtaining a new life is currently governed by a real-time regeneration wait, restricting when the player can continue playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards indicator and boundary rule: daily reward progress resets when the player misses a day.",
      "rationale": "The game offers daily prizes and penalizes a missed day by restarting the reward sequence."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be dismissed normally: there is no close control, tapping the ad redirects to the store, returning to the game leaves the ad stuck, and the player must terminate the game to escape. This is a forced or effectively unskippable ad, supporting Advertisement. The missing close control and taps being routed toward the store obstruct the opt-out and direct attention toward a purchase destination, supporting UI Misdirection. The text does not describe an actual purchase prompt caused by insufficient funds or a default purchase action, so Easy to Purchase is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "Forced to watch ads or given rewards for watching ads",
      "rationale": "The advertisement cannot be dismissed through the normal interface, effectively forcing the player to remain in or terminate the ad experience."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary rule: an obstructed opt-out or absent close control, especially where interaction routes toward a purchase destination",
      "rationale": "The interface removes the close option and makes taps lead to the store, obstructing the player's attempt to opt out and directing them toward a commercial destination."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

