}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy-gated progression system. Once the player's energy is depleted, further progress is blocked until the in-game timer restores the energy, and the reviewer says that recovery takes a long time. This is an imposed regeneration wait that requires the player to return according to the game's timer. The review does not state that money can remove the wait, so Pay to Progress is not assigned. The comments about limited content, desired console play, storyline, maps, and customization are requests or evaluations rather than dark-pattern mechanics.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers impose arbitrary waits, including energy regeneration, before continued play or progress.",
      "rationale": "Progress is blocked when energy runs out and can resume only after waiting for the game's recovery timer."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes capture attempts consuming Poké Balls unusually quickly after the player began purchasing them, creating a need to buy more. This supports a payment-linked progression or continuation barrier: the player must purchase additional consumable capture attempts to keep capturing. The claim that Pokémon keep breaking out specifically after purchases alleges that the game manipulates or tunes capture outcomes to increase resource consumption, which fits Illusion of Control as an alleged hidden determination of outcomes. The final edit says the review was posted without the account holder writing it and denies the claimed conversations, describing an unauthorized review under the player's identity; this supports Friend Spam / Impersonation under the store-review destination exception. No currency conversion, gambling, reward-delivery, or social-recruitment mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Pay to Progress applies where payment is required to continue or advance through gameplay, including when consumable resources needed for continued play must be purchased.",
      "rationale": "The player says that faster depletion of capture balls makes purchasing more necessary to continue capturing Pokémon."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out",
      "rule_applied": "boundary rule vs alleged rigging; outcome manipulation or tuning attributed to the game qualifies even when the reviewer frames it as a suspicion.",
      "rationale": "The span alleges that capture outcomes are manipulated after purchases, causing repeated breakouts and concealing the game's true outcome determination."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW",
      "rule_applied": "Destination of the message rule; an installed product posting a store review under the player's account is in scope.",
      "rationale": "The account holder states that the review was posted in their name without their authorship, implying unauthorized impersonation through the game's review activity."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a time-limited in-game event with a visible two-day window. The player was close to earning a 525-dice reward but, after running out of dice and returning from sleep, found that the event had changed before they could finish it. This makes the player play according to the event's schedule and lose access to a nearly completed reward when the game clock changes. The review does not explicitly describe payment being required, repeated tasks, daily login rewards, or pressure to continue playing to avoid falling behind beyond this specific event deadline.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed",
      "rule_applied": "Playing by Appointment boundary rule: where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The event's game-set schedule changed while the player was away, causing the player to lose the opportunity to complete it and claim the reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a referral mechanic that is supposed to reward inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook, which supports Forced Fellowship even though the reviewer says the rewards are no longer granted. It also describes chance-based gameplay: the player stakes a large amount of dice through a max-bet action, receives no successful shutdowns or bank heists, and repeatedly gets duplicate stickers. The max-bet and luck language supports Gambling using an in-game stake, while the repeated duplicate sticker outcomes support Reward Mania because rewards are delivered through an unpredictable pool. The inability to log in is explicitly attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a technical fault and does not support a dark-pattern label. No real-money purchase, collection compulsion, daily reward cycle, or progress gate is explicitly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator",
      "rationale": "The game uses rewards to incentivize the player to recruit friends into the game through Facebook."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK!",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling boundary: a chance mechanic with an in-game currency stake qualifies even when no real-money purchase is described.",
      "rationale": "The player describes staking a large amount of dice on a max-bet chance action and receiving an unsuccessful outcome."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator; duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomized pool",
      "rationale": "The repeated duplicate stickers indicate an unpredictable reward-delivery system that keeps producing unwanted outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an interface that automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-bet 1v1 table, directing the player toward a risky option instead of a neutral or low-bet choice. The reviewer says this causes accidental mis-clicks and loss of accumulated coins. It also states that, after losing those coins, players are forced to buy more with real money, establishing a real-money-to-virtual-currency conversion. The 1v1 section describes competition against other players. The requested default lock and lowest-table behavior are hypothetical additions and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "Exciting UI Effect / UI Misdirection boundary: the interface directs the player toward a selected option and away from safer alternatives.",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically focuses the player on the highest-bet table, steering attention and selection toward a costly option."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "This is a cheap \"Dark UX\" trick to force accidental mis-clicks",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases: visual design or default options deceive players into unintended spending or transactions.",
      "rationale": "The default interface setup is described as causing unintended taps that make the player enter a high-cost table."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency / Intermediate Currency: real money is used to acquire in-game coins.",
      "rationale": "The wording implies that players must replace lost virtual coins by purchasing them with real money."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "go to the 1v1 section",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "A 1v1 section is a game mode in which the player competes directly against another player."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a random-number-based outcome system that the player believes is deliberately unfavorable: \"the rng is so obviously against you\" asserts that outcomes are being manipulated rather than determined fairly, which fits Illusion of Control. The reviewer also mentions spending actual money and a level costing 2200, but does not clearly state that payment is required to progress, that real money buys in-game currency, or that a specific amount of currency is stranded or unusable. \"you'll barely make that back\" describes poor reward value rather than a realized leftover or concrete shortfall, so it does not support Waste Aversion. No other mechanic is explicitly established.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game manipulates or rigs random outcomes against the player, rather than merely reporting an unlucky run."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game with repetitive gameplay, but “Same old same old grinder” is only a general evaluation and does not name a repeated activity tied to progress, so it does not support T_Grinding. The review explicitly describes events as effectively unwinnable for ordinary players unless they spend money, including a stated $30k expenditure to win the last event. This supports Pay to Progress through the Pay to Win indicator. The reference to winning an event also describes competitive event play, supporting Competition. No currency conversion, random reward mechanic, daily schedule, or other coded mechanic is explicitly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win. Calculated total spent on last event was 30k to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money; also the definition of Pay to Progress requiring money to progress or win.",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly states that spending money is the route to winning the event and reports spending 30k to do so."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes players competing in an event with a winner."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several active game mechanics. It explicitly characterizes the game as pay-to-win, which indicates that spending money provides an in-game advantage. Base updates use increasingly long timers, imposing waits on progression. The statement that everything scales endlessly and offers no meaningful endpoint describes an indefinitely expanding progression treadmill, although “more grind” does not identify a specific repetitive activity and therefore does not independently support Grinding. The UI is said to be deliberately confusing and overwhelming in order to push purchases, which is UI Misdirection. Alliances create competitive and coercive multiplayer dynamics: players outside alliances are attacked and bullied, so the game makes players compete against one another. This does not establish Encourages Anti-Social Behavior because the review does not describe a reward flowing to players for harmful conduct, and the coerced-alliance boundary does not support Forced Fellowship when grouping is sought for protection without social obligation.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review states that paying players gain an advantage in the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that impose arbitrary waits",
      "rationale": "Base upgrades are governed by increasingly long timers that delay continued progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to, just more grind",
      "rule_applied": "definition; game progression continually expands without an attainable end state",
      "rationale": "Endless scaling and the absence of a meaningful endpoint imply that the game continually extends rather than being completable."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary rule: interface design that directs or obstructs the player in service of a purchase",
      "rationale": "The review directly attributes confusing interface design to steering players toward buying."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "definition; the game makes players compete against other players",
      "rationale": "Players are placed in adversarial relations where non-allied players are attacked by others."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily-reward system that requires the player to place a 50,000 bet before the daily rewards can be completed. The player also describes timed rewards with a maximum payout, but this is an amount limit rather than a wait imposed on gameplay. The daily cycle is a standalone reward opportunity, not a gate on overall progression, so Daily Rewards applies without Playing by Appointment. The bet is not explicitly tied to real money or purchased currency, so Gambling and Pay to Progress are not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards definition and boundary rule: assign when a daily reward mechanism has an active requirement or consequence affecting completion; do not add Playing by Appointment when it does not gate overall progression.",
      "rationale": "The game makes completion of the daily-reward cycle conditional on placing a 50,000 bet."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

